#: src/macros/kinds/attr.md:6
msgid "Attribute Macros"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/attr.md:8
msgid ""
"**Attribute macros** are attached as custom attributes to any standard Rust "
"`item` (including functions, modules, structs, etc.). They have full power "
"to inspect, rewrite, or completely replace the item they are attached to."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/attr.md:13
msgid "// A popular example from the Tokio library:\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/attr.md:16
msgid "\"Hello from an async main!\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/attr.md:20
msgid "**Universal:** Can be applied to any declaration/item."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/attr.md:21
msgid ""
"**Transformational:** Unlike derive macros, they can entirely replace their "
"target with different code."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/attr.md:23
msgid ""
"**Parameters:** Can optionally accept custom metadata parameters, e.g., "
"`#[route(GET, \"/\")]`."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/attr.md:25
msgid "**Implementation:** Always implemented as procedural macros."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/attr.md:29
msgid ""
"Discuss how `#[tokio::main]` converts an `async fn main()` into a "
"synchronous `fn main()` that instantiates and runs the Tokio runtime, blocks "
"on the future, and executes the async block. This completely rewrites the "
"function signature!"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/attr.md:33
msgid ""
"Emphasize that because attribute macros can entirely rewrite items, they are "
"extremely powerful but require careful error handling to avoid completely "
"obscuring compile errors for the user."
msgstr ""
